ic_multiplicity_006_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&2&1\\\\0&0&2\\end{pmatrix}$, what are $\\operatorname{am}(2)$ and $\\operatorname{gm}(2)$?","options":["$\\operatorname{am}(2)=3$ and $\\operatorname{gm}(2)=2$","$\\operatorname{am}(2)=3$ and $\\operatorname{gm}(2)=3$","$\\operatorname{am}(2)=3$ and $\\operatorname{gm}(2)=1$","$\\operatorname{am}(2)=2$ and $\\operatorname{gm}(2)=2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1f8e0e28ca15d6032903113d","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_algebraic_multiplicity_018_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The eigenvalue $2$ of a matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has $\\operatorname{am}(2)=3$. Which statement is NOT guaranteed by this?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{A}$ has three linearly independent eigenvectors for $2$","$(\\lambda-2)^3$ divides the characteristic polynomial of $\\boldsymbol{A}$","$\\boldsymbol{A}$ has at least one eigenvector for $2$","$\\boldsymbol{A}$ has at least $3$ rows and $3$ columns"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c0cb46a10f27ab4db2a9b487","time_limit":35}]}}